; mini.mx -- the grammar of a small imperative language, and no target at all.
;
; Every rule below emits by calling one procedure and does nothing else. What a
; construct *is* is in this file; what it is *written as* is in a backend file
; beside it, lib/mini-c.mx or lib/mini-python.mx, and neither of them is named
; here. The target is chosen where the file is read rather than where it is
; written:
;
;     mx -u lib/mini.mx -u lib/mini-c.mx      -i prog.mini    ; C
;     mx -u lib/mini.mx -u lib/mini-python.mx -i prog.mini    ; Python
;
; Nothing had to be built for this. `-u` may be given more than once
; (REFERENCE §9), a rule may call a template that arrived in another file
; because calls are resolved once the whole header is sealed (§3.8), and a
; parameter carries its hole's level, so `group(a, 60)` answers inside a
; backend and each target decides its own brackets. Without that last one the
; split would be cosmetic: the grammar would be making a decision about C.
;
; Two things are deliberately not here, and docs/ROADMAP.md 15 is the entry:
;
;   the separator     `=> "\n"` below joins statements with a line break and
;                     nothing else, and every procedure emits its own
;                     terminator, C's `;` included. A backend *can* take the
;                     output separator instead, with `@separator … override`,
;                     but only if it is named after this file on the command
;                     line, where a call is order-free. One of the two orders
;                     working is worse than neither, so the terminator is the
;                     statement's here.
;
;   `terminated`      belongs to a template (§3.4) and there is one template
;                     per rule here, so it could not vary by target anyway.
;                     Nothing above needs it, because nothing is joined after
;                     a statement but a newline.
;
; And what this file cannot say: which procedures it requires. A backend that
; is missing one is `no template called '…'` at the call site, which is a good
; message and not a contract.

@comment "#" eol

@token number "[0-9]+"
@token name   "[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*"

@separator ";" => "\n"

@syntax "program" "{" b:stmts "}"           => { program(b) }

@syntax "let" n:name "=" v                  => { let(n, v) }
@syntax "set" n:name "=" v                  => { set(n, v) }
@syntax "print" x                           => { print(x) }
@syntax "if" c "then" "{" b:stmts "}"       => { iff(c, b) }
@syntax "while" c "do" "{" b:stmts "}"      => { loop(c, b) }

@syntax a "<" b   40                        => { lt(a, b) }
@syntax a "+" b   60                        => { add(a, b) }
@syntax a "-" b   60                        => { sub(a, b) }
@syntax a "*" b   70                        => { mul(a, b) }
@syntax "(" e ")" 95                        => { paren(e) }
